This is Telldus Core version {version}

Telldus Core is the driver and tools for controlling a Telldus Technologies
TellStick. It does not containing any GUI tools which makes it suitable for
server use.


INSTALLING Telldus Core

On Windows, if you want to install the precompiles binary packages, simply
launch the package and follow the instructions in the installation wizard.

If you have a source package (a .tar.gz file), follow the instruction in the
INSTALL file.


CONFIGURATION AND TOOLS

Once Telldus Core is installed, we suggest that you start by adding the devices
you want to control.

On Windows, this is done by installing TelldusCenter. On Linux it's done by
editing the file /etc/tellstick.conf directly, or in TelldusCenter.

Telldus Core installs the tool tdtool for controlling devices with TellStick.
Have a look in the man page for a description how to use it:

	man tdtool

HEADLESS LINUX BUILD

The Linux headless build uses this directory, telldus-core/, as the CMake
source root. The Phase 1 headless boundary is the daemon target telldusd, the
shared library target telldus-core, and the command-line target tdtool.

TelldusCenter, Qt GUI components, bindings, examples, Docker runtime setup,
Raspberry Pi-specific packaging, MQTT, and Home Assistant integration are
outside this headless build boundary.

Prerequisites for the basic headless configure/build proof are:

	- CMake
	- C and C++ compiler toolchain
	- pthreads / CMake Threads
	- libconfuse
	- libftdi/libusb stack
	- pkg-config

From the repository root, configure the headless build with:

	cmake -S telldus-core -B build/telldus-core-headless \
	  -DFORCE_COMPILE_FROM_TRUNK=TRUE \
	  -DBUILD_TDTOOL=TRUE \
	  -DBUILD_TDADMIN=FALSE \
	  -DENABLE_TESTING=FALSE \
	  -DFTDI_ENGINE=libftdi

Then prove the headless target graph with:

	cmake --build build/telldus-core-headless --target telldusd telldus-core tdtool -- -n

The dry build command is intentionally non-installing and does not require a
TellStick to be connected. Full Arch Linux build/test fixing continues in
Phase 2.

